    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That sounds a bit informal for a real office setting. I'm more concerned about the documentation side of it. Could you explain how a manager should actually record these steps to make sure everything is fair and legally sound if we eventually have to let someone go?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Of course, I understand the need for a more formal approach. In a typical three-step progressive discipline process, documentation is key to ensuring fairness and legal compliance. Here's how you can document each step:\n\n1. **First Step: Verbal Warning**\n   - Record the date, time, and location of the meeting.\n   - Note the employee's name and position.\n   - Document the specific performance issues or rule violations observed.\n   - Outline the expectations and the desired improvements.\n   - Record the employee's response and any agreed-upon action plan.\n   - Ensure the employee acknowledges the warning and understands the consequences of further issues.\n\n2. **Second Step: Written Warning**\n   - Document the date, time, and location of the meeting.\n   - Include a summary of the previous verbal warning and any subsequent performance or behavior.\n   - Clearly state the specific performance issues or rule violations.\n   - Outline the expectations and the desired improvements once again.\n   - Record the employee's response and any agreed-upon action plan.\n   - Ensure the employee acknowledges the written warning and understands the consequences of further issues.\n\n3. **Third Step: Final Warning or Termination**\n   - Document the date, time, and location of the meeting.",
      "source": "target",
